WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.699
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday, it's very
early because I have to go to New York this

00:00:03.699 --> 00:00:04.699
morning.

00:00:04.699 --> 00:00:08.029
So here's a funny thing that happened, Hank:
Time Magazine named me one of the one hundred

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:13.170
most influential people in the world, so I'm
going to New York to go to this party.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:17.390
Now, I am aware - how do I say this without
sounding ungrateful - I am aware that this

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:20.220
is an unearned honor.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.460
Like I can personally name several thousand
people who are more influential than I am

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.500
who did not make the Time 100.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.760
There's Liberian President, Ellen Johnson
Sirleaf, the actress and activist Laverne

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.500
Cox, the artist Ai Weiwei.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.649
Also every single morning French President
Francois Hollande wakes up and thinks, "You

00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:41.180
know, I think today I won't use France's nuclear
arsenal to start a war that would end the

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:42.180
human species."

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.140
And that is an extremely influential decision.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.780
But that noted, I am delighted and honored
to have been included, and now I get to go

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.440
to this party with other people on the Time
100.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.149
Like, maybe Vladimir Putin and Kim Il Sung.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:55.170
Probably not Kim Il Sung.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:01:00.239
However, if he is there, I promise I will
take a selfie with him and then I will make

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:02.429
a citizen's arrest for human rights violations.

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:05.780
One more thing about the Time 100 before I
get to the actual topic of today's video,

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.460
they called me a teen whisperer.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.860
Which made me think about what if I were an
actual teen whisperer.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.900
Like, "Hey teens, where are we gonna go to,
like, take drugs and snapchat?"

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.820
But it's true, Hank, I know what the teens
love to talk about, Pulitzer Prize winning

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.320
non-fiction books about people living in a
slum in Mumbai.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.720
Alright, I have to go to the airport, but
you should come with me.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:23.720
Movie magic!

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.220
So Hank, this summer in the Nerdfighter bookclub
we're reading Behind the Beautiful Forevers

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.670
by Katherine Boo, which is just a fascinating
book.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.720
But then at first I realized that I didn't
give you a due date, which is just terrible

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.510
teachering on my part; I apologize; so, uh,
the movie The Fault In Our Stars comes out

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:38.950
on June 6th , things are gonna be a little
crazy until then, so June 10th.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:42.690
You must read this book by June 10th or else
nothing is going to happen to you.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.490
Also, if you're the kind of student who wants
extra credit, and let's face it Nerdfighters,

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:49.229
you probably are, then I think it's really
interesting to read Behind the Beautiful Forevers

00:01:49.229 --> 00:01:52.220
- we need an acronym for it, like TFIOS...

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:53.220
BtBF.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.310
Yes, BtBF - Anyway, I think it's really interesting
to read BtBF in the context of another book

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.050
about Mumbai: Maximum City.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:00.050
So that's your extra credit assignment.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.860
Okay, so no spoilers but I want to talk about
a few things to kinda bear in mind while you're

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.180
reading Behind the Beautiful Forevers.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.709
First, I think it's really important to resist
the urge to extrapolate, like this is a book

00:02:08.709 --> 00:02:13.439
about a few people living in one community;
it's not a book about slums in general or

00:02:13.439 --> 00:02:14.439
India in general.

00:02:14.439 --> 00:02:15.459
Also, luck.

00:02:15.459 --> 00:02:17.060
Hank, we like stories that make sense.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.579
I mean that's part of the reason we tell each
other stories, part of the reason why we read

00:02:19.579 --> 00:02:23.450
novels and watch movies, and we're hardwired
to like stories that make sense because human

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:28.030
memory itself is narrative, so there's like
a luck-eraser inside the human mind that tells

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:30.870
you that Z happened because of X and Y.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:34.049
But actually, Z happened because of X and
Y and lots of luck.

00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:38.549
Luck of birth and genetics and circumstance
and some editor at Time Magazine liking your

00:02:38.549 --> 00:02:39.549
book!

00:02:39.549 --> 00:02:42.060
So how does our view of the world change when
we incorporate luck into it instead of assuming

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.540
that human life is like essentially a meritocracy?

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:45.540
Okay a couple other things.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.099
The idea of private property, that's weird,
how does land become owned?

00:02:49.099 --> 00:02:50.099
Also water.

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:51.379
It's always worth paying attention to water.

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:54.550
And lastly, when we talk about global poverty
we usually talk about people who live on the

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:57.010
equivalent of less than $1.25 a day.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.040
That's the case for more than a billion people.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.769
But it's not the case for the people Katherine
Boo writes about, who are living in Annawadi.

00:03:02.769 --> 00:03:06.769
The people living in Annawadi live above the
global poverty line and they're participating

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:09.329
and - and some would say benefiting from - global
capitalism.

00:03:09.329 --> 00:03:13.260
But of course what that actually means in
their lives turns out to be very complicated.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.920
And Hank, that's the great thing about Behind
the Beautiful Forevers.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:21.040
Everything is complicated, governments, non-profit
organizations, micro finance, religious organizations,

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.470
complicated, people, complicated - it's so
good!

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.159
Alright my flight is boarding so I gotta go.

00:03:26.159 --> 00:03:28.390
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:29.390
Global international poverty -

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.073
Loudspeaker announcement: May I have your
attention please.

00:03:31.073 --> 00:03:32.073
J: You can have my attention.

00:03:32.073 --> 00:03:35.269
Speaker: Transportation security administration
mandates the removal of unattended -

00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:36.439
J: Does it REALLY?!

00:03:36.439 --> 00:03:38.269
I've never heard that before!

